WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.679
Cloud.md is how Cloud Code gets persistent instructions, and it's organized as a hierarchy

2
00:00:05.679 --> 00:00:11.039
at four scopes. For this one, we'll cover those scopes, what each one is designed for,

3
00:00:11.039 --> 00:00:16.639
how Cloud Code loads them all at session start, and why separating personal preferences from

4
00:00:16.639 --> 00:00:22.079
shared project standards is the core design principle behind the whole system. Let's get into

5
00:00:22.079 --> 00:00:28.079
it. Open a session in any repo, and Cloud Code is already reading instructions from more than

6
00:00:28.079 --> 00:00:34.799
one place. Managed policy is an org-wide Cloud.md at a system path. It loads first,

7
00:00:34.799 --> 00:00:40.319
it's the broadest layer, and it can't be excluded. That's how an organization enforces standards for

8
00:00:40.319 --> 00:00:46.880
everyone. User scope is .cloud slash Cloud.md, your personal preferences across every session.

9
00:00:47.439 --> 00:00:54.319
Project scope is Cloud.md at the repository root, the shared instructions for all contributors,

10
00:00:54.319 --> 00:00:59.840
version controlled, so everyone gets the same setup. There's a fourth and narrowest scope,

11
00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:07.279
local, which is Cloud.local.md, and get ignored, and we'll come back to it. The key idea is load

12
00:01:07.279 --> 00:01:12.879
order. Cloud Code reads these broadest to most specific and concatenates them into one set of

13
00:01:12.879 --> 00:01:18.400
instructions. Nothing gets erased. They all apply together, and on a genuine conflict,

14
00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:24.639
there is no reliable winner, so avoid contradictions. Managed policy is the broadest

15
00:01:24.639 --> 00:01:31.760
layer, and it loads first. It's an org-wide Cloud.md placed at a system path by the organization,

16
00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:37.760
and its defining property is that it can't be excluded. Every user in every project inherits

17
00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:44.319
it before any personal or project config loads. That makes it the mechanism for enforcing standards

18
00:01:44.319 --> 00:01:50.800
across a whole company, security requirements, compliance language, house style that has to hold

19
00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:56.000
everywhere. This is the key correction to internalize. Organization-wide enforcement

20
00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:02.160
is managed policy, not a shared project file and not a team-level file type. If a rule truly has

21
00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:07.440
to apply to everyone and can't be opted out of, managed policy is the only scope that guarantees

22
00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:14.000
it. It sets the floor the rest of the hierarchy builds on. These two scopes sit between managed

23
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:21.679
policy and local. User scope is .cloud slash cloud.md, your personal defaults across every

24
00:02:21.679 --> 00:02:28.240
project you touch. Project scope is the cloud.md at the repository root, and it's the workhorse

25
00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:33.919
of the hierarchy. It applies to everyone who works on that project. It's the right place for coding

26
00:02:33.919 --> 00:02:39.119
conventions the team has agreed on, test requirements, architecture guidelines, and

27
00:02:39.119 --> 00:02:45.440
anti-patterns you want cloud code to avoid. The most important detail for project scope

28
00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:53.360
is version control. The project cloud.md should be committed to source control alongside the code,

29
00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:59.199
so every contributor automatically gets the same instructions with no manual setup. Leaving it out

30
00:02:59.199 --> 00:03:05.839
of source control is a concrete anti-pattern. A cloud.md that lives on one developer's machine

31
00:03:05.919 --> 00:03:11.360
but isn't in the repo produces inconsistent results and defeats the point of a shared config.

32
00:03:12.639 --> 00:03:18.240
The four boxes here all answer one question. Who is this instruction for? Match the instruction

33
00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:23.440
to its audience and placement stops being a guess. Anything the whole company answers to

34
00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:29.919
belongs in managed policy, including security requirements and standards nobody can opt out of.

35
00:03:29.919 --> 00:03:35.279
Anything that is purely yours, your preferred tone, your verbosity, your own shortcuts,

36
00:03:35.279 --> 00:03:41.279
belongs at user scope. Anything the team has to stay consistent on belongs at project scope,

37
00:03:41.839 --> 00:03:47.360
and anything that is yours but scoped to one repository belongs in your git ignored local file.

38
00:03:47.919 --> 00:03:51.759
The trap is putting a personal preference where the whole team inherits it,

39
00:03:51.759 --> 00:03:57.119
or a team standard where only you ever see it. Audience first, then scope.

40
00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:04.639
Let's close out the scopes with the narrowest one. Local scope is clod.local.md and it's git

41
00:04:04.639 --> 00:04:10.320
ignored so it never leaves your machine. Use it for the tweaks that apply to one repository

42
00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:15.039
but shouldn't be committed for the whole team. Things like a scratch directory path, a local

43
00:04:15.039 --> 00:04:20.000
database name, or notes on how your own machine is set up. There's one more distinction worth

44
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:26.480
drawing here. You can share a clod.md across projects by importing it or symlinking it,

45
00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:31.760
and that's a real technique. But sharing and enforcing are different things. An import can

46
00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:37.760
be edited or deleted locally by anyone holding the file, so it spreads config without ever

47
00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:45.200
guaranteeing it. When you open a session, clod code finds and loads all applicable clod.md files

48
00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:50.480
at the same time. Every scope applies simultaneously, and the load order runs

49
00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:56.880
managed policy first, then user, then project, then local. If a user scope instruction and a

50
00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:02.480
project scope instruction don't conflict, both simply apply. The model follows both.

51
00:05:02.480 --> 00:05:07.920
When guidance genuinely conflicts, there's no reliable winner, so the fix is to avoid writing

52
00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:15.040
rules that contradict each other. The other key detail here is context window cost. Every clod.md

53
00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:21.920
file that gets loaded counts against the session context window. A verbose project, clod.md with

54
00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:27.839
hundreds of lines of detail shrinks the working space available for code. Keep config files

55
00:05:27.839 --> 00:05:34.799
concise and specific. Clod.md is a standing instruction file that you author by hand,

56
00:05:34.799 --> 00:05:40.559
not an auto-updating memory store. It's loaded fresh at every session start and counts against

57
00:05:40.559 --> 00:05:47.200
the context window from the start. You manage it like code. It doesn't rewrite itself as you work.

58
00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:52.160
That's an important distinction because clod code does have a separate auto-memory system.

59
00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:58.559
It lives in your home directory, keyed to the git repo you're in, and stays on your machine

60
00:05:58.559 --> 00:06:03.760
rather than being committed to the repo. It persists notes across sessions on its own.

61
00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:09.600
So the two serve different jobs. Clod.md is where you put standing instructions and keep them lean.

62
00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:16.160
The memory system is where evolving project notes accumulate over time. Treat clod.md like a

63
00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:20.880
configuration file, not a journal, and let the memory system carry the running notes.

64
00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:28.320
The hierarchy exists so a single file doesn't have to serve an entire organization, a person,

65
00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:35.279
and a project all at once. Each layer answers a different question about who the rule belongs to,

66
00:06:35.279 --> 00:06:41.920
and putting a rule at the wrong layer is how team standards end up unenforceable. Keep in mind,

67
00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:46.880
they all load at session start, so every layer costs a little bit of your context window.

68
00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:50.160
That's it for this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

